# Caddyfile — static origin for mastermind-x.com, behind the TencentEdgeOne CDN
# (migrated from Cloudflare — the CF-specific notes below now mean "the CDN edge").
#
# The CDN terminates public TLS at its edge and connects back to this origin.
# `tls internal` serves Caddy's self-signed cert on :443 so the edge<->origin hop
# is still encrypted; the CDN's origin-fetch/SSL mode must ACCEPT a self-signed
# cert (Cloudflare's "Full", or EdgeOne's equivalent non-strict HTTPS origin pull).
# To upgrade later, replace `tls internal` with a CDN Origin Certificate
# (`tls /path/cert.pem /path/key.pem`) and switch the edge to strict verification.
#
# HTTPS ENFORCEMENT: Force-HTTPS is the EDGE's job — enable "Force HTTPS" + HSTS in
# the EdgeOne console. Cloudflare used to redirect http->https AND auto-upgrade
# stray mixed content; EdgeOne does neither by default, so that toggle is now what
# stops http://mastermind-x.com from serving plaintext. This origin deliberately
# does NOT redirect (an origin redirect behind a CDN can loop the edge's origin
# pull — see disable_redirects below); it only ADDS the HSTS response header on the
# site block, which is safe to emit from behind the CDN and hardens return visits.
# Until the edge toggle is on, :80 serves the SAME site as :443 (mirror block
# below) — never Caddy's implicit empty 200, which the edge can cache for the
# httpS URLs too (the 2026-07-11 /china.html poisoning; scheme-less cache key).
#
# Pre-launch: the whole site is served but marked noindex so it can't be crawled
# before launch. Product login (Supabase) arrives in Slice 2; for a private beta
# right now, front this with Cloudflare Access (email OTP, zero code).
{
	# The CDN edge owns the public http->https redirect for EVERY host in this file;
	# don't fight it with Caddy's automatic upgrade, which can loop the edge's origin
	# pull. disable_redirects suppresses only the AUTO upgrades — the explicit http://
	# blocks below (the www :80 mirror, the admin redirect, the port-80 421 catch-all)
	# still route normally.
	#
	# There is NO grey-cloud exception, despite what this comment claimed until
	# 2026-08-07. admin.* is edge-proxied like the rest: it CNAMEs to
	# admin.mastermind-x.com.eo.dnse3.com -> 43.159.99.101 / 43.159.98.106, the same
	# EdgeOne edge IPs as apex/www/app/bot, and the edge Force-HTTPS 302s
	# http://admin.* before this box ever sees it (all five hosts probed on :80,
	# 2026-08-07 — every one answered 302 with `Server: TencentEdgeOne`). So the
	# explicit http:// blocks now serve exactly ONE path: a direct-to-origin :80 hit
	# that bypasses DNS, which ufw permits from Anywhere. Still worth keeping for
	# that path (see the admin block), but no longer what upgrades a real visitor.
	auto_https disable_redirects

	# NOT "the real visitor" — this said "trust Cloudflare's forwarded client IP",
	# which is not what the directive does. private_ranges trusts RFC1918 peers only,
	# and the edge arrives from PUBLIC addresses (43.175.104.x observed on
	# origin-pull), so it is deliberately UNTRUSTED: {remote_host}, {client_ip} and
	# the access log all resolve to the EDGE, not the client. That default is the safe
	# one, because ufw lets anyone reach this box directly: a caller can forge ANY
	# forwarding header. `EO-Connecting-IP` is the ONLY one the edge overwrites on
	# EVERY zone this box serves; `True-Client-IP`, `X-Real-IP` and `CF-Connecting-IP`
	# arrive carrying whatever the caller sent, everywhere.
	#
	# `EO-Client-IP` IS ZONE-DEPENDENT — do not read a claim about it as estate-wide.
	# #4922 probed admin.* and found it absent from a clean request and forwarded
	# verbatim when forged (the EdgeOne "Client IP Header" rule is not active there).
	# Re-probing www.* on 2026-08-07 found the OPPOSITE: the rule IS active, a clean
	# request carries it, and a forged copy arrives overwritten with the true client.
	# Both measurements are real; they describe different zones. A resolver that reads
	# it is correct on one host and silently forgeable on the other, which is why
	# `app/edge_client.py` reads only the header that holds on both.
	#
	# Consequence before you key anything on a peer IP: per-client keying off
	# {remote_host} buckets ALL edge-borne traffic into one. That bit the admin login
	# lockout, and it is FIXED in `admin/edge_trust.py` (resolve behind an ATTESTED
	# edge peer, fall back to the peer otherwise) — NOT by reading a header here.
	# The public app needs no such attestation, because on www the edge does write a
	# true per-visitor `EO-Connecting-IP`; `app/edge_client.py` explains why routing
	# www through `from_edge` would RE-CREATE the admin lockout DoS rather than fix
	# anything. Read whichever of the two modules serves the host you are editing
	# before adding a `header_up` anywhere in this file.
	servers {
		trusted_proxies static private_ranges
	}
}

# Shared serving body for mastermind-x.com — imported by BOTH the https site
# block and the plain-HTTP mirror below it, so the two schemes can never drift
# apart. EdgeOne's GET cache key IGNORES the scheme (verified live 2026-07-11
# with a throwaway ?query key): whatever port 80 answers for a path can be
# served from cache to httpS visitors — so :80 must serve the real site with
# the real Cache-Control, never Caddy's implicit empty 200.
(mastermind_static) {
	# Root is /opt/macro/site.served, NOT the git work-tree /opt/macro/site.
	# macro-update rsyncs site/ -> site.served (atomic per-file rename) so a
	# `git reset --hard` refresh can never expose a half-written 0-byte file to be
	# cached by the CDN edge (the 2026-07-03 white-page incident). Paired with the
	# short must-revalidate Cache-Control below as defence-in-depth.
	root * /opt/macro/site.served
	encode zstd gzip

	# Slice 2 backend — FastAPI (macro-api.service) on localhost. Everything under
	# /api/* is proxied here; the rest is served as static files below. Per-user
	# auth lives in the API (Supabase) + (later) a login wall; no shared password.
	#
	# RV W2: the Research Vault's /api/research/* routes (catalog, search, view,
	# download, quota) are ALREADY covered by this /api/* proxy — no extra block is
	# needed. The private research PDFs live ONLY in the private R2 bucket and are
	# fetched server-side by the authed API; they have NO public URL and MUST NOT
	# gain a public static-file rule here (no *.pdf in @public_static below) — the only
	# path to a PDF is the require_user + paid + rate-limited/metered API route.
	# Pre-parse body cap for the ONE public unauthenticated write surface
	# (POST /api/support/ticket, SEE W1). The app also refuses an oversized
	# declared Content-Length, but that check is bypassable with chunked
	# transfer-encoding — the app never sees a length to refuse. Only the edge can
	# enforce a real cap, because only the edge sits before the body is read.
	#
	# Scoped to /api/support/* on purpose, NOT the whole /api/* tree: a global cap
	# here would also apply to the Stripe webhook (invoice objects with long line-item
	# lists) and the /api/collect analytics batch (up to 40 events), and truncating
	# either to fix a contact form would be a bad trade.
	request_body /api/support/* {
		max_size 64KB
	}

	# This paid endpoint authenticates before app-level decoding.  Cap it at the
	# edge as well, including chunked requests that lack Content-Length.
	request_body /api/biocatalyst/v1/trial-peer-sets:resolve {
		max_size 16KB
	}

	reverse_proxy /api/* 127.0.0.1:8000 {
		# TRUSTED peer identity for per-client rate limiting. mastermind-x.com is
		# behind EdgeOne, so every real-client header the app reads (EO-Client-IP,
		# CF-Connecting-IP, X-Forwarded-For, …) is ATTACKER-SUPPLIABLE at the origin:
		# a bot that rotates EO-Client-IP per request lands every hit in a fresh
		# rate-limit bucket. header_up REPLACES any inbound X-MM-Peer with the real
		# TCP peer, so this one header cannot be spoofed (same mechanic as
		# X-Admin-Client-IP on the admin host below). app/support.py rate-limits on
		# BOTH keys — the claimed IP tightly, the trusted peer loosely (CN traffic
		# legitimately aggregates behind a handful of EdgeOne edge IPs).
		#
		# The eo-*/cf-* headers are deliberately NOT scrubbed: EdgeOne sets them for
		# real in CN routing and other consumers (analytics geo, the site gate) read
		# them. This adds a trustworthy signal rather than removing untrusted ones.
		# WHICH of them the app may believe is decided in app/edge_client.py — probing
		# www through the live edge on 2026-08-07 found EO-Connecting-IP overwritten
		# with the true client while True-Client-IP, X-Real-IP and CF-Connecting-IP
		# arrived carrying the forged values a caller sent. Read that module before
		# adding a consumer of any eo-*/cf-* header.
		header_up X-MM-Peer {remote_host}
	}

	# Live Tape websocket — same-origin relay to macro-api (app/tape.py).
	# China-reachable (same-origin, like the /sb Supabase proxy). Placed with
	# the /api/* proxy so a ws upgrade never reaches the HTML gate matchers.
	reverse_proxy /ws/tape 127.0.0.1:8000 {
		# Same trusted peer as /api/*, for the same reason: app/tape.py caps
		# concurrent sockets per client, and without this header its resolver has no
		# unspoofable rung between the edge's EO-Connecting-IP and the 127.0.0.1
		# socket peer — which is Caddy for every visitor, i.e. one shared bucket.
		header_up X-MM-Peer {remote_host}
	}

	# ── Supabase auth/REST proxy — the GFW hole ───────────────────────────────
	# *.supabase.co is fronted by CLOUDFLARE (verified: `server: cloudflare`,
	# 172.64.149.246 / 104.18.38.10), whose ranges are throttled in mainland China.
	# Everything ELSE here was hardened for that years ago — supabase.js is vendored
	# same-origin, fonts are self-hosted, no CDN <script> survives — but the auth
	# ENDPOINT was never brought inside the perimeter, so a China visitor loads the
	# site through EdgeOne and then fails every auth call: sign-in, sign-up, password
	# reset, magic link. (The "/sb Supabase proxy" the /ws/tape comment above refers
	# to did not exist until this block; probing the live host returned the site
	# gate's 401 for both /sb/* and /auth/v1/*.)
	#
	# handle_path strips the /sb prefix, so browser_url = https://www.mastermind-x.com/sb
	# and supabase-js's own ${url}/auth/v1/… concatenation lands on the real paths.
	# The droplet is DigitalOcean (outside China) and makes the supabase.co hop.
	#
	# This grants the browser NOTHING it did not already have — it could call the
	# same project directly with the same public anon key; RLS is what isolates
	# users, and it is unchanged. Two things to know:
	#   * every proxied request reaches Supabase from the DROPLET's IP, so any
	#     per-IP rate limiting upstream now sees one client. X-Forwarded-For is
	#     forwarded (Caddy default) so gotrue can still bucket per real client.
	#   * auth responses must never be cached — no-store below, and EdgeOne needs a
	#     matching cache-bypass rule for /sb/* (docs/ops/site-access.md).
	handle_path /sb/* {
		reverse_proxy https://fsldfzlxyavsuwqbceod.supabase.co {
			header_up Host fsldfzlxyavsuwqbceod.supabase.co
			header_down Cache-Control "no-store"
		}
	}

	# VPS live plane — artifacts are owned by systemd lanes and published by
	# atomic rename outside both the git work-tree and site.served. This prevents
	# a main-branch pull/rsync from rolling a fresh tick back to the last commit.
	#
	# SECURITY: quotes, breadth, and official release lifecycle are explicitly
	# reviewed PUBLIC assets. Every other /live/* artifact and the heatmap remain
	# inside @reg_asset below and therefore pass registration + paywall checks
	# before the external file is considered. Never add a top-level /live/*
	# file_server: that would bypass the default-deny static boundary.
	@vps_public_live {
		path /live/quotes.json /live/intraday_quotes.json /live/flow_pulse.json /live/breadth.json /live/release_publications.json /live/staleness.json
		file {
			root /var/lib/macro-live/public
		}
	}
	handle @vps_public_live {
		root * /var/lib/macro-live/public
		header Cache-Control "no-store"
		file_server
	}
	# Zero-downtime fallback before live-setup creates the external quote.
	handle /live/quotes.json {
		header Cache-Control "public, max-age=60, must-revalidate"
		file_server
	}
	handle /live/breadth.json {
		header Cache-Control "public, max-age=60, must-revalidate"
		file_server
	}
	handle /live/release_publications.json {
		header Cache-Control "no-store"
		file_server
	}
	handle /live/staleness.json {
		header Cache-Control "no-store"
		file_server
	}

	# Protected external artifacts are served only from inside @reg_asset's
	# authenticated route. The matcher also provides a zero-downtime fallback:
	# when a file is absent externally, the canonical site.served file wins.
	@vps_external {
		path /live/* /marketdata/sp500_heatmap.json
		file {
			root /var/lib/macro-live/public
		}
	}

	# ── Bare-path aliases (backlink + crawler hygiene) ────────────────────────
	#
	# Extensionless URL guesses — /blog, /start, /plans — carry no ".html" and no
	# trailing slash, so they miss BOTH the *.html document matcher and the
	# /blog/* style prefix exclusions below. They therefore landed in @reg_asset
	# and died at the registration wall's 401 JSON: a crawler or an inbound link
	# got a hard auth error where a page exists. These 301 them onto the canonical
	# sitemap form instead.
	#
	# `redir` sorts BEFORE `handle`/`file_server` in Caddy's default directive
	# order, so these resolve ahead of the Site Access Boundary below (this snippet
	# is imported at the top level of the site block — it is NOT wrapped in an
	# explicit `route`, which would swap ordering to written order). Redirecting
	# onto a regwalled .html target is intentional and safe: the gate still runs on
	# the redirected request.
	#
	# Section hubs -> the canonical /<section>/index.html the sitemap lists.
	redir /blog /blog/index.html 301
	redir /blog/ /blog/index.html 301
	redir /learn /learn/index.html 301
	redir /learn/ /learn/index.html 301
	redir /products /products/index.html 301
	redir /products/ /products/index.html 301
	redir /tools /tools/index.html 301
	redir /tools/ /tools/index.html 301
	redir /stocks /stocks/index.html 301
	redir /stocks/ /stocks/index.html 301
	redir /research /research/index.html 301
	redir /research/ /research/index.html 301
	# Top-level pages -> the same name with its extension.
	redir /start /start.html 301
	redir /plans /plans.html 301
	redir /macro /macro.html 301
	redir /about /about.html 301
	redir /support /support.html 301
	redir /privacy /privacy.html 301
	redir /terms /terms.html 301
	redir /disclaimer /disclaimer.html 301
	redir /us_stocks /us_stocks.html 301
	redir /research_vault /research_vault.html 301
	redir /confluence_screener /confluence_screener.html 301
	# Consolidated-away page with real Google demand (63 impressions pre-outage):
	# #4299 merged baskets_china into sector_central_china and left a meta-noindex
	# redirect stub. A server-side 301 consolidates the URL's equity onto the
	# canonical successor instead of burning it in a noindex hop (SEO Supercharge
	# W1b-redux ruling, research/SEO_SUPERCHARGE_MASTERPLAN_BY_FABLE.md).
	redir /baskets_china.html /sector_central_china.html 301
	# Daily Movers is no longer a standalone product surface. Its gainers, losers
	# and same-direction theme read now live on the canonical US stock library.
	# Keep the fragment in the Location header so old bookmarks land on the exact
	# consolidated section rather than at the top of a long dossier directory.
	redir /movers.html "/stocks/index.html#today-movers" 301

	# ── Site Access Boundary ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
	#
	# DEFAULT-DENY STATIC SERVING. Only the explicit public funnel/assets listed
	# in config/site_access.yml bypass authentication. Every other file — HTML,
	# JSON, data-bearing JS, images, generated model outputs, and future unknown
	# extensions — passes two independent fail-closed checks:
	#   1. /api/regwall/check  — valid Supabase account.
	#   2. /api/paywall/check  — when PAYWALL_ENABLED=1, active/trialing
	#                            entitlement carrying feature `site_full`.
	#
	# Documents also pass the pre-existing fail-open IP/country gate FIRST. The
	# public landing/plans funnel retains the one-page-still-up behavior. Product
	# paths never fail open: if either auth service is unavailable, Caddy returns
	# a lock/503 response and NEVER falls through to file_server.
	#
	# Keep PUBLIC-BOUNDARY below byte-aligned with config/site_access.yml. The
	# hermetic boundary test rejects drift.

	# Internal previews/QA and proprietary research tools are not customer
	# content, even for entitled accounts. The seven research tools are available
	# only through admin.mastermind-x.com's authenticated /research-tools/ route.
	@never_site {
		path /_mockup_ccw_credit_desk.html /_mockup_research_vault.html /qa_bottom_sensors.html /status.html /committee.html /measurement.html /crossasset.html /signal_lab.html /tech_lab.html /macro_signals.html /factors.html
	}
	handle @never_site {
		header Cache-Control "private, no-store"
		header X-Robots-Tag "noindex, noarchive"
		respond "Not found" 404
	}

	# ── HTML documents are OPEN (operator 2026-08-04) ─────────────────────────
	#
	# The registration wall used to 302 every non-funnel *.html to
	# /?signin=1&ret=<path>, so an unregistered visitor — and Googlebot, which
	# never has a session — bounced off ~239 member pages. That wall is gone:
	# EVERY page shell is now readable by anyone, and the @reg_html matcher that
	# used to enforce it has been retired along with its handler.
	#
	# What did NOT change, and is the whole reason this is safe: the paid product
	# is not the page, it is the PAYLOAD. @reg_asset below is unchanged and still
	# default-deny for every non-HTML path, so the graded rows keep their gates:
	#   * premium.enforced_early — /premiumdata/*, /capital-structure-data/*,
	#     /allocationdata/special_situations.json, /chinaspecialdata/special.json
	#     — 403 for anonymous AND Free, ahead of the PAYWALL_ENABLED switch;
	#   * every per-ticker <market>stockdata/*.json graded emit;
	#   * every other data payload not named in config/site_access.yml.
	# So an anonymous visitor gets the shell, the methodology and the honest
	# totals, and tier_preview.js paints the locked slots + upgrade wall where the
	# paid rows would be. That is the house tier-preview pattern
	# (docs/TIER_PREVIEW_PATTERN.md) applied estate-wide instead of page by page.
	#
	# Internal surfaces are NOT affected: @never_site above still 404s the
	# mockups, QA and research-tool pages, and it is matched before this block.

	# Protected non-HTML and extensionless paths. This is the closure of the
	# original leak: direct JSON/JS/model-output URLs no longer bypass auth.
	# PUBLIC-BOUNDARY-START
	@reg_asset {
		not path /api/* /sb/* /ws/tape / /index.html /plans.html /confluence_screener.html /macro.html /start.html /us_stocks.html /research_vault.html /research_vault_app.js /market_memory.html /biocatalyst.html /biocatalyst.css /biocatalyst.js /about.html /special_situations.html /china_special_situations.html /etfs.html /china_heatmap.html /support.html /unsubscribe.html /privacy.html /terms.html /disclaimer.html /favicon.svg /favicon.ico /apple-touch-icon.png /robots.txt /88bb90b05303e3cf469878ebc4dc7543.txt /sitemap.xml /llms.txt /brand-facts.json /onboard.css /onboard.js /tier_preview.css /tier_preview.js /landing.css /scene-motion.css /scene-motion.js /chat.css /theme.css /market_memory.css /market_memory.js /navigation-refresh.css /product-nav-icons.css /logo_config.js /stock-logos.js /theme.js /dashboard-icons.css /dashboard-icons.js /chart_i18n.js /timemachine.js /tablesort.js /charts.js /risk_state_live.js /release_publications_live.js /heatmap.js /stocktable.js /globe-deck.js /sky.js /hub-welcome.js /vendor/d3-array.min.js /vendor/d3-geo.min.js /vendor/topojson-client.min.js /world-110m.json /adtest.js /account.js /nav_market.js /supabase.js /data_base.js /live.js /live_config.js /live/quotes.json /live/intraday_quotes.json /live/flow_pulse.json /live/breadth.json /live/release_publications.json /live/staleness.json /prophet/showcase.json /seasonalitydata/methodology.json /seasonalitydata/index.json /seasonalitydata/entities/SPY.json /marketdata/china_heatmap.json /factordata/tech_lab.json /stock_seasonality.css /stock_seasonality.html /stock_seasonality.js /fundamental_forensics.css /fundamental_forensics.js /lightweight-charts-v5.js /chart.js /stockview.js /stockbrief.js /mtf.js /aidesk_lean.js /mm_brain.js /plotly-2.32.0.min.js /lightweight-charts.js /cycle.css /sector_cycles.css /macro-desk.css /markets.css /odds.css /capital_structure.css /government-revenue-parity.css /illus.css /illus.js /watchlist.js /watchstore.js /market_books.js /portfolio.js /portfolio_state.js /portfolio_import.css /portfolio_import.js /portfolio_import_ui.js /assets/css/* /assets/js/* /assets/landing/* /factordata/tech_events/* /stocks/* /products/* /tools/* /learn/* /blog/* /research/* /fonts/* *.html
	}
	# PUBLIC-BOUNDARY-END
	handle @reg_asset {
		route {
			reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8000 {
				method GET
				rewrite /api/regwall/check
				header_up X-Original-Uri {http.request.uri}
				header_up X-Original-Kind asset
				@rallow status 2xx
				handle_response @rallow {
				}
			}
			reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8000 {
				method GET
				rewrite /api/paywall/check
				header_up X-Original-Uri {http.request.uri}
				header_up X-Original-Kind asset
				@pallow status 2xx
				handle_response @pallow {
				}
			}
			header Cache-Control "private, no-store"
			header Vary "Cookie"
			header X-Robots-Tag "noindex, noarchive"
			handle @vps_external {
				root * /var/lib/macro-live/public
				file_server
			}
			file_server {
				index index.html
			}
		}
	}

	@gate_html {
		# Public funnel only — IP gate retains its fail-open availability contract.
		path / /index.html /plans.html /confluence_screener.html /macro.html /start.html /us_stocks.html /research_vault.html /market_memory.html /stock_seasonality.html /about.html /special_situations.html /china_special_situations.html /etfs.html /china_heatmap.html /support.html /unsubscribe.html /privacy.html /terms.html /disclaimer.html /stocks/* /products/* /tools/* /learn/* /blog/* /research/*
	}

	handle @gate_html {
		route {
			# Ask the gate about THIS request. `method GET` + `rewrite` turn the
			# sub-request into GET /api/gate/check while preserving the visitor's
			# headers (EO-Client-IPCountry / EO-Connecting-IP / CF-*) so the gate
			# can read them.
			reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8000 {
				method GET
				rewrite /api/gate/check

				# 2xx (204 allow): empty handle_response → CONTINUE to file_server.
				@allow status 2xx
				handle_response @allow {
				}
				# Non-2xx (403 block): the default copy_response streams the gate's
				# body (the self-contained coming-soon page) + its no-store/X-Gate
				# headers back to the visitor and STOPS — so the max-age header
				# below never touches the block response (it keeps no-store).
			}
			# Reached ONLY on allow — serve the file with the normal short HTML TTL.
			header Cache-Control "public, max-age=60, must-revalidate"
			file_server {
				index index.html
			}
		}
	}

	# Every remaining *.html — the ~239 pages the registration wall used to 302.
	# Same fail-open IP/country gate as the funnel above, then the file.
	#
	# These are READABLE but deliberately still NOT INDEXABLE: opening the wall is
	# an access decision, and handing Google 239 new URLs in the same change would
	# be an SEO decision nobody made. The X-Robots-Tag below preserves the posture
	# these pages already had while they were gated. Dropping the header (or
	# folding these paths into @gate_html above) is the one-line follow-up that
	# opts them into the index — do it deliberately, per page family, with the
	# sitemap (lib/seo.py) updated in the same change.
	@open_html {
		path *.html
		not path /api/* /sb/* /ws/tape
	}

	handle @open_html {
		route {
			reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8000 {
				method GET
				rewrite /api/gate/check
				@allow status 2xx
				handle_response @allow {
				}
			}
			header Cache-Control "public, max-age=60, must-revalidate"
			header X-Robots-Tag "noindex, noarchive"
			file_server {
				index index.html
			}
		}
	}

	handle_errors {
		# NOTE: the @reg_html_err block that used to live here is GONE. It was the
		# thing that actually emitted `302 -> /?signin=1&ret=<path>`: HTML
		# documents were fail-CLOSED, so an unregistered visitor (or a gate-upstream
		# blip) bounced off the page instead of reading it. HTML is no longer
		# registration-gated, so there is nothing left to fail closed ON, and a
		# missing/erroring page must surface as its real status code.
		#
		# Assets still fail closed below — that is deliberate and is what keeps the
		# paid payloads from leaking on an upstream failure.

		# Protected assets fail CLOSED too. The JSON response is intentionally
		# small and non-cacheable; never substitute the requested static file.
		#
		# /live/wires.json and /live/intelligence.json are listed below for a
		# DIFFERENT reason than the public entries beside them: they stay
		# registration-gated in @reg_asset above, but a MISSING file must reach the
		# client as a real 404. Without the exclusion, file_server's 404 enters
		# handle_errors and is rewritten to the 503 "temporarily unavailable" body
		# — so before either daemon artifact exists, every registered visitor would
		# be told the desk is broken instead of the module simply staying hidden.
		# Excluding them here cannot leak a file: the error path has no file_server
		# (asserted by
		# tests/test_regwall_json_gate.py), so content is still only ever served
		# from inside `handle @reg_asset` after the gate returns 2xx.
		@reg_asset_err {
			not path /api/* /sb/* /ws/tape / /index.html /plans.html /confluence_screener.html /macro.html /start.html /us_stocks.html /research_vault.html /research_vault_app.js /market_memory.html /biocatalyst.html /biocatalyst.css /biocatalyst.js /about.html /special_situations.html /china_special_situations.html /etfs.html /china_heatmap.html /support.html /unsubscribe.html /privacy.html /terms.html /disclaimer.html /favicon.svg /favicon.ico /apple-touch-icon.png /robots.txt /88bb90b05303e3cf469878ebc4dc7543.txt /sitemap.xml /llms.txt /brand-facts.json /onboard.css /onboard.js /tier_preview.css /tier_preview.js /landing.css /scene-motion.css /scene-motion.js /chat.css /theme.css /market_memory.css /market_memory.js /navigation-refresh.css /product-nav-icons.css /logo_config.js /stock-logos.js /theme.js /dashboard-icons.css /dashboard-icons.js /chart_i18n.js /timemachine.js /tablesort.js /charts.js /risk_state_live.js /release_publications_live.js /heatmap.js /stocktable.js /globe-deck.js /sky.js /hub-welcome.js /vendor/d3-array.min.js /vendor/d3-geo.min.js /vendor/topojson-client.min.js /world-110m.json /adtest.js /account.js /nav_market.js /supabase.js /data_base.js /live.js /live_config.js /live/quotes.json /live/intraday_quotes.json /live/flow_pulse.json /live/breadth.json /live/release_publications.json /live/wires.json /live/intelligence.json /prophet/showcase.json /seasonalitydata/methodology.json /seasonalitydata/index.json /seasonalitydata/entities/SPY.json /marketdata/china_heatmap.json /factordata/tech_lab.json /stock_seasonality.css /stock_seasonality.html /stock_seasonality.js /fundamental_forensics.css /fundamental_forensics.js /lightweight-charts-v5.js /chart.js /stockview.js /stockbrief.js /mtf.js /aidesk_lean.js /mm_brain.js /plotly-2.32.0.min.js /lightweight-charts.js /cycle.css /sector_cycles.css /macro-desk.css /markets.css /odds.css /capital_structure.css /government-revenue-parity.css /illus.css /illus.js /watchlist.js /watchstore.js /market_books.js /portfolio.js /portfolio_state.js /portfolio_import.css /portfolio_import.js /portfolio_import_ui.js /assets/css/* /assets/js/* /assets/landing/* /factordata/tech_events/* /stocks/* /products/* /tools/* /learn/* /blog/* /research/* /fonts/* *.html
		}
		handle @reg_asset_err {
			header Cache-Control "private, no-store"
			header Content-Type "application/json"
			header X-Robots-Tag "noindex, noarchive"
			respond `{"error":"site_access_temporarily_unavailable"}` 503
		}

		# Public funnel only: retain fail-open availability if the IP gate is down.
		@gate_html_err {
			path / /index.html /plans.html /confluence_screener.html /macro.html /start.html /us_stocks.html /research_vault.html /market_memory.html /stock_seasonality.html /about.html /special_situations.html /china_special_situations.html /etfs.html /china_heatmap.html /support.html /unsubscribe.html /privacy.html /terms.html /disclaimer.html /stocks/* /products/* /tools/* /learn/* /blog/* /research/*
		}
		handle @gate_html_err {
			header Cache-Control "no-store"
			file_server {
				status 200
				index index.html
			}
		}
	}

	# Only explicit public assets can reach this direct file server.
	file_server {
		index index.html
	}

	# --- CDN cache safety (2026-07-03 white-page incident) ----------------------
	# A response with NO Cache-Control gets EdgeOne's LONG default TTL, so any
	# transient bad body would be STUCK at the edge for hours. A short,
	# must-revalidate TTL caps any bad edge entry (HTML <=60s, other static <=300s)
	# and forces the edge to revalidate against the (healthy) origin. Mirrors the
	# greydeercapital.com block below. The zero-window structural fix (atomic
	# swap + flock + 0-byte rsync guard) lives in update.sh.
	#
	# NOTE: HTML's max-age=60 is now set INSIDE the Site Access Gate handle above
	# (on the allow path only), so a blocked visitor's coming-soon page keeps its
	# Cache-Control: no-store instead of being clobbered to a cacheable 60s. Do not
	# re-add a blanket `header @html Cache-Control` here — it would re-cache blocks.
	#
	# Only reviewed PUBLIC assets receive shared-cache headers. A blanket
	# extension matcher here would override a protected response's no-store and
	# turn the CDN into an authentication bypass.
	@public_static {
		path /favicon.svg /favicon.ico /apple-touch-icon.png /robots.txt /88bb90b05303e3cf469878ebc4dc7543.txt /sitemap.xml /llms.txt /brand-facts.json /onboard.css /onboard.js /tier_preview.css /tier_preview.js /landing.css /scene-motion.css /scene-motion.js /chat.css /theme.css /market_memory.css /market_memory.js /biocatalyst.css /biocatalyst.js /navigation-refresh.css /stock-logos.js /logo_config.js /product-nav-icons.css /theme.js /dashboard-icons.css /dashboard-icons.js /chart_i18n.js /timemachine.js /tablesort.js /charts.js /risk_state_live.js /release_publications_live.js /heatmap.js /stocktable.js /globe-deck.js /sky.js /hub-welcome.js /vendor/d3-array.min.js /vendor/d3-geo.min.js /vendor/topojson-client.min.js /world-110m.json /adtest.js /account.js /nav_market.js /supabase.js /data_base.js /live.js /live_config.js /research_vault_app.js /prophet/showcase.json /seasonalitydata/methodology.json /seasonalitydata/index.json /seasonalitydata/entities/SPY.json /marketdata/china_heatmap.json /stock_seasonality.css /stock_seasonality.js /lightweight-charts-v5.js /chart.js /stockview.js /stockbrief.js /mtf.js /aidesk_lean.js /mm_brain.js /plotly-2.32.0.min.js /lightweight-charts.js /cycle.css /sector_cycles.css /macro-desk.css /markets.css /odds.css /capital_structure.css /government-revenue-parity.css /illus.css /illus.js /watchlist.js /watchstore.js /market_books.js /portfolio.js /portfolio_state.js /portfolio_import.css /portfolio_import.js /portfolio_import_ui.js /assets/css/* /assets/js/* /assets/landing/* /fonts/*
		not query v=*
	}
	header @public_static Cache-Control "public, max-age=300, must-revalidate"

	# Temporary Terminal ingest carve-outs. They are explicit public policy
	# entries and can be removed when that separate flow authenticates.
	@factordata_public {
		path /factordata/tech_lab.json /factordata/tech_events/*
	}
	header @factordata_public Cache-Control "public, max-age=300, must-revalidate"

	# Reviewed public assets carrying ?v=<hash> are safe to cache forever: the
	# hash is derived from the file body, so a change yields a NEW URL and a stale
	# body cannot be stuck at the edge under a reused name.
	# scripts/optimize_assets.py stamps ?v= on every local .js/.css post-render, so
	# the shared bundle (theme.js/heatmap.js/theme.css, ~143KB) stops being
	# re-revalidated on every page navigation — the dominant mobile cost. Refs
	# WITHOUT ?v= (e.g. a page rendered before the sweep) keep the short TTL above.
	#
	# THIS LIST MUST TRACK @public_static. optimize_assets stamps EVERY local
	# .js/.css ref, not a curated set, so a stamped asset missing here matches
	# @public_static's `not query v=*` too and ends up with NO Cache-Control at
	# all — which hands EdgeOne its long default TTL, the exact 2026-07-03
	# white-page incident class this section exists to prevent. Measured
	# 2026-08-20 before the sweep below: 38 reviewed-public assets and 20,525
	# page-refs were landing in that hole, /navigation-refresh.css (9,644 refs)
	# worst. tests/test_site_access_boundary.py now fails if the lists drift.
	#
	# Adding a path here is NOT an access decision and must never widen one: every
	# entry is already reviewed public — either listed in @public_static above or
	# carried in config/site_access.yml `public.exact`. Member-only assets
	# (wh_banner.js, mm_charts.js, sector_cycles.js, …) answer 401 + no-store and
	# MUST stay out, or the CDN caches a gated body under a public key.
	@public_versioned {
		path /onboard.css /onboard.js /landing.css /scene-motion.css /scene-motion.js /chat.css /theme.css /market_memory.css /market_memory.js /biocatalyst.css /biocatalyst.js /product-nav-icons.css /theme.js /release_publications_live.js /adtest.js /account.js /nav_market.js /supabase.js /data_base.js /live.js /live_config.js /assets/css/* /assets/js/* /assets/landing/* /navigation-refresh.css /stock-logos.js /logo_config.js /lightweight-charts-v5.js /chart.js /plotly-2.32.0.min.js /lightweight-charts.js /macro-desk.css /tablesort.js /illus.js /illus.css /chart_i18n.js /charts.js /dashboard-icons.css /cycle.css /dashboard-icons.js /stockview.js /sector_cycles.css /heatmap.js /tier_preview.css /stocktable.js /tier_preview.js /timemachine.js /risk_state_live.js /vendor/topojson-client.min.js /vendor/d3-geo.min.js /vendor/d3-array.min.js /stockbrief.js /stock_seasonality.js /stock_seasonality.css /sky.js /research_vault_app.js /odds.css /markets.css /hub-welcome.js /globe-deck.js /capital_structure.css /government-revenue-parity.css /aidesk_lean.js /mtf.js /mm_brain.js
		query v=*
	}
	header @public_versioned Cache-Control "public, max-age=31536000, immutable"

	# The watchlist funnel-shell scripts arrive ?v=N-stamped, but those stamps
	# are HAND-AUTHORED integers in watchlist.html.j2 (W0 packet §2.9), not
	# content hashes — so they can take neither @public_static above (its
	# `not query v=*` skips them) nor @public_versioned's immutable year (a body
	# edit without a stamp bump would pin the stale body at the edge forever).
	# Verified 2026-08-20 (post-A1A commissioning fix wave, Sol post-review F8):
	# watchlist.js?v=13, watchstore.js?v=10, market_books.js?v=3, portfolio.js?v=10,
	# portfolio_state.js?v=1 plus A1B's portfolio_import.{css,js}?v=1 and
	# portfolio_import_ui.js?v=1 — integers,
	# and none equals its own sha256[:8]. mtf.js and mm_brain.js LEFT this list the same day: both are
	# served content-hash-stamped (b62aea2f / f74045d8, each matching its file
	# body), so the immutable year is correct for them and the 300s here was
	# costing a revalidation on every navigation. Membership is a claim about
	# how a path is STAMPED, not about who owns it — re-check the served stamp
	# before adding one back.
	# Without this matcher their stamped responses carry NO Cache-Control at
	# all, which hands EdgeOne its long default TTL — the 2026-07-03 white-page
	# incident class. Same short TTL as their unstamped form.
	@watchlist_shell_versioned {
		path /watchlist.js /watchstore.js /market_books.js /portfolio.js /portfolio_state.js /portfolio_import.css /portfolio_import.js /portfolio_import_ui.js
		query v=*
	}
	header @watchlist_shell_versioned Cache-Control "public, max-age=300, must-revalidate"

	header {
		# PUBLIC (was pre-launch noindex): the site is now open to search engines.
		# The old `X-Robots-Tag "noindex, nofollow, noarchive"` header was removed
		# here so crawlers can index it (paired with robots.txt + sitemap.xml in
		# site/). Re-add that header to pull the whole site back out of search.
		# Basic hardening.
		X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"
		X-Frame-Options "DENY"
		X-Permitted-Cross-Domain-Policies "none"
		Referrer-Policy "strict-origin-when-cross-origin"
		Permissions-Policy "camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=(), usb=()"
		# Minimal enforceable CSP that does not break the legacy inline-script
		# pages. Tightening script-src/style-src needs a nonce migration.
		Content-Security-Policy "base-uri 'self'; object-src 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'"
		-Server
	}
}

www.mastermind-x.com {
	import mastermind_static

	header {
		# Enforce HTTPS on return visits (the edge should set this too). 1 year,
		# matching admin.*; no includeSubDomains — other subdomains manage their own.
		Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000"
	}

	# Self-signed origin cert; pair with the CDN's non-strict HTTPS origin pull.
	tls internal

	# Access logs go to journald (`journalctl -u caddy`). We deliberately do NOT
	# write a file log: the stock caddy.service systemd sandbox only permits writes
	# under /var/lib/caddy, so a /var/log/caddy file sink fails with EACCES and
	# crashes startup. journald is the idiomatic, sandbox-safe sink.
}

# ---- apex mastermind-x.com — 301 to the CDN-fronted www ---------------------
# Split out of the www block by #3040 (2026-07-19). The SPLIT is still correct:
# keeping the apex out of the www serving block is what stops EdgeOne's www
# origin-pull (Host: www.mastermind-x.com) from landing on this redirect and
# looping. #3040's DNS/TLS rationale was wrong when written; corrected below,
# all of it re-verified live 2026-08-07.
#
# * The apex is a CNAME, NOT an A record: mastermind-x.com ->
#   mastermind-x.com.eo.dnse3.com -> 43.159.98.106 / 43.159.99.101, the same
#   EdgeOne edge IPs as www. Every estate host (apex/www/admin/app/bot) is
#   proxied this way — pages.yml recorded it 2026-07-05, two weeks BEFORE #3040
#   claimed the apex pointed straight here. Nothing migrated after #3040; the
#   comment simply mis-described a topology that was already in place.
# * Public TLS for the apex is terminated AT THE EDGE (TrustAsia DV, EdgeOne's
#   CA). Visitors never see this box's cert. The Let's Encrypt cert Caddy holds
#   is what the ORIGIN presents on EdgeOne's origin-pull (and to direct-to-IP
#   hits) — real and in use, but not the public-facing cert.
# * ACME HTTP-01 still works, but NOT "on :80" as #3040 said — the edge's
#   Force-HTTPS 302s the challenge away before it ever reaches this box. It
#   survives that hop: LE follows the 302 to https://mastermind-x.com/..., the
#   edge origin-pulls it here, and Caddy answers the challenge ahead of routing.
#   Verified by GETting the challenge path through the edge and watching this
#   box's handler fire ("looking up info for HTTP challenge", identifier
#   mastermind-x.com) in `journalctl -u caddy`. NB no renewal has been exercised
#   since the apex moved behind the edge — the cert is still the original
#   2026-06-30 issuance and ARI selects 2026-08-29 05:42 UTC for the first one.
#   Watch that run. If it ever fails, the fix is `tls internal` here (origin-pull
#   is non-strict, exactly like www above), NOT a DNS revert.
# * The MX hazard #3040 feared has NOT materialised. The apex still carries
#   `10 m.northlighttrade.com` and it resolves alongside the apex CNAME (28/28
#   across Google/Cloudflare/Quad9/OpenDNS/114DNS/AliDNS) because DNSPod answers
#   the apex per-qtype instead of chasing its own CNAME. That is still RFC 1034
#   §3.6.2 non-conformant, and transient CNAME-only/NODATA answers for MX (and
#   even for A) did show up during that testing — so treat it as a live caution,
#   not a closed question. Reverting the apex to an A record is the remedy IF
#   mail ever flaps; nothing else needs it, ACME included.
http://mastermind-x.com {
	redir https://www.mastermind-x.com{uri} permanent
}

mastermind-x.com {
	header Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000"
	redir https://www.mastermind-x.com{uri} permanent
}

# ---- plain-HTTP (:80) mirror — the 2026-07-11 /china.html poisoning fix ------
# With `auto_https disable_redirects` and https-only site blocks, Caddy answered
# ANY plain-HTTP request for these hosts with its IMPLICIT catch-all: an empty
# 200 with no body and no Cache-Control. EdgeOne's origin pull follows the
# visitor's scheme (Force-HTTPS off at the edge), so one bot GET of
# http://mastermind-x.com/china.html primed the edge with that empty 200 —
# and because the edge cache key ignores the scheme, httpS visitors got the
# dead page for hours (no Cache-Control -> EdgeOne's long default TTL).
# Serving the REAL site on :80 means an http origin pull can only ever cache a
# real page bounded by max-age=60. Deliberately NOT a redirect: if the edge's
# origin-pull were ever http-only, an origin http->https redirect would loop
# (see the header notes above) — the edge console owns Force-HTTPS.
http://www.mastermind-x.com {
	import mastermind_static
}

# ---- plain-HTTP catch-all — kill Caddy's implicit empty 200 -------------------
# Every OTHER hostname that reaches this box over :80 (app./bot./greydeer/
# slate/maltese/stray Host headers) used to fall into the same implicit empty
# 200 — the exact cacheable-poison shape above, just waiting for a CDN in front
# of any of those hosts. A 421 with no-store is never cacheable, so the port-80
# path can't poison anything again. Explicit http:// blocks (the mastermind
# mirror above, the admin redirect below) outrank this hostless block, and
# Caddy answers ACME HTTP-01 challenges before routing, so admin.* cert
# issuance is unaffected.
http:// {
	header Cache-Control "no-store"
	respond "Misdirected: use https://" 421
}

# ---- app.mastermind-x.com — the Mastermind Terminal (Next.js) ----------------
# The charting SaaS frontend, served by terminal.service (next start) on :3000.
# Needs a Cloudflare DNS A record: app -> 146.190.142.17 (proxied), SSL mode "Full".
app.mastermind-x.com {
	encode zstd gzip

	# --- Terminal /data static serving (2026-07-03) ------------------------------
	# Serve the terminal's static data JSONs DIRECTLY from disk at Caddy, bypassing
	# Next.js: `next start` 404s public/ files added AFTER the build (verified live:
	# a rsync'd NEW <SYM>.fund.json 404'd until rebuild), and the fundamentals
	# pipeline rsyncs NEW per-symbol files daily (fund/opts/tx waves). Caddy's
	# file_server has no build manifest — a new file serves on the next request.
	# Cache headers set here (plain header: file_server emits no Cache-Control, so
	# no defer needed). EdgeOne still RefreshHits until an owner console rule exists.
	handle /data/* {
		root * /opt/terminal/terminal/public
		@manifest path /data/manifest.json
		@rest {
			path /data/*
			not path /data/manifest.json
		}
		header @manifest Cache-Control "public, s-maxage=60, max-age=0, must-revalidate"
		header @rest Cache-Control "public, s-maxage=300, max-age=0, must-revalidate"
		file_server
	}

	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:3000
	header {
		X-Robots-Tag "noindex, nofollow, noarchive"
		-Server
	}
	# Self-signed origin cert; pair with Cloudflare SSL mode = "Full".
	tls internal
}

# ---- bot.mastermind-x.com — Brain BOT desk, PRO-ONLY (uvicorn :8001) ----------
# The Mastermind Brain dashboard (paper-trading FastAPI), served by
# mastermind.service on 127.0.0.1:8001. The app's browser login flow is gone; its
# remaining auth covers only mutating/LLM-triggering operator POSTs (bearer
# token), so until this block gained a gate every PAGE and read API on the desk
# answered 200 to anyone. MUST live here — without a site block, Caddy's `tls
# internal` has no cert for this SNI and the Cloudflare<->origin handshake fails
# with HTTP 525. DNS A record: bot -> 146.190.142.17 (proxied), SSL mode "Full".
#
# THE GATE. Every path except /health is a Pro-membership surface. Identity is
# the shared Supabase session cookie (Domain=.mastermind-x.com, so a request to
# this host carries it), resolved by macro-api's /api/paywall/check_pro — a
# WHO-only sibling of /api/paywall/check that deliberately does NOT classify the
# path: config/site_access.yml describes the macro site's URL space, and this is
# a different application's. 2xx continues to the origin; on anything else
# Caddy's default copy_response streams the 403 interstitial back and STOPS, so
# the bot app is never reached. Fail-closed by construction: if macro-api is
# down, unreachable, or answering 404, nobody gets in.
#
# DEPLOY-ORDER WINDOW (accepted, self-healing). app/deploy/update.sh reinstalls
# and reloads Caddy near the top of its run but only restarts macro-api much
# later, after runtime setup and pip reconciliation. On the single tick that
# ships (or later changes) this endpoint, Caddy therefore calls a route the
# still-running macro-api does not have yet: 404, non-2xx, deny — every visitor
# including Pro members sees the upgrade interstitial for roughly a minute, then
# the macro-api restart heals it. That is the correct direction to fail, so the
# ordering is left alone; do NOT "fix" it by treating 404 as allow.
#
# /health is handled FIRST and stays ungated on purpose: admin/uptime_board.py
# probes it over the PUBLIC hostname and needs an unauthenticated 200 to tell
# "the service is down" apart from "you are not Pro" — a gated probe collects an
# anonymous 403 and cannot see an outage. (The bot repo's own deploy script hits
# 127.0.0.1:8001/health directly for its rollback check, so it never passes
# through here; keeping this hole open is for the public-side monitor.)
bot.mastermind-x.com {
	encode zstd gzip
	header {
		X-Robots-Tag "noindex, nofollow, noarchive"
		-Server
	}

	handle /health {
		reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8001
	}

	handle {
		route {
			reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8000 {
				method GET
				rewrite /api/paywall/check_pro
				header_up X-Original-Uri {http.request.uri}
				@proallow status 2xx
				handle_response @proallow {
				}
			}
			# A Pro-only body must never sit in a shared cache. This host is
			# orange-clouded, so Cloudflare is the shared cache in question.
			header Cache-Control "private, no-store"
			header Vary "Cookie"
			reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8001
		}
	}

	# Self-signed origin cert; pair with Cloudflare SSL mode = "Full".
	tls internal
}

# ---- admin.mastermind-x.com — the Admin console (python -m admin :8787) -------
# Authenticated control + observability console (admin.service on 127.0.0.1:8787).
# The app enforces password + signed-session auth (ADMIN_PASSWORD in
# /etc/macro-admin.env); Caddy adds anti-framing + HSTS headers.
#
# NOT GREY-CLOUD. This block claimed for months that admin.* was a direct A record
# with no CDN in front, and the global options block above repeated it. Both were
# wrong. Corrected and re-verified live 2026-08-07:
#
# * admin is a CNAME to admin.mastermind-x.com.eo.dnse3.com -> 43.159.99.101 /
#   43.159.98.106 — the same EdgeOne edge IPs as apex/www/app/bot. No host in this
#   estate is grey-cloud; :80/:443 being open to Anywhere in ufw (the
#   firewall-cloudflare.sh escape hatch) is what makes the direct path reachable,
#   not evidence that DNS points here.
# * Public TLS is terminated AT THE EDGE — TrustAsia DV (EdgeOne's CA), Jun 30 ->
#   Sep 27 2026. Visitors never see the certificate this box holds.
# * The Let's Encrypt cert Caddy provisions (YE1, Jun 30 -> Sep 28 2026) is real and
#   in use, but only on the edge's origin-pull and on direct-to-IP hits. Keeping it
#   over `tls internal` is fine — origin-pull is non-strict either way.
#
# ACME HTTP-01 SURVIVES THE EDGE HOP, password gate and all. NOT "on :80 ahead of
# this redirect" as this comment used to say — :80 never reaches the box for a real
# client. LE follows the edge's 302 to https://admin.mastermind-x.com/.well-known/…,
# the edge origin-pulls it here, and Caddy's solver answers AHEAD of routing, so
# neither the admin password gate nor the reverse_proxy below can shadow it; the
# edge does not gate /.well-known/acme-challenge/* on this host either. Verified by
# GETting the challenge path through the edge and watching the handler fire in
# `journalctl -u caddy` (the 401 the probe gets back is the admin app answering
# after the solver falls through — the journal line is the evidence, not the body):
#   "looking up info for HTTP challenge" identifier=admin.mastermind-x.com
#   remote_addr=43.175.104.236 error="no information found to solve challenge…"
# "no information found" just means no order was in flight; the point is that the
# request REACHED the solver, from an edge IP. Zero ACME errors for this host in the
# journal, and Caddy polls LE's ARI for it roughly every 6h.
#
# NB NO RENEWAL HAS BEEN EXERCISED since admin moved behind the edge — the cert is
# still the original 2026-06-30 issuance, so a clean journal is not proof the next
# one works. ARI selects 2026-08-29 20:13 UTC (window 08-28 12:07 -> 08-30 07:18);
# the apex's first lands the same week. Watch both. If either fails, the fix is
# `tls internal` here (origin-pull is non-strict, exactly like www), NOT a DNS
# revert — TLS-ALPN-01 genuinely is dead behind the edge, so HTTP-01 is the only
# live path and must not be removed.
#
# WHY THE http:// BLOCK BELOW STILL EARNS ITS KEEP, now that its original reason is
# gone. It no longer fires for real visitors: the edge Force-HTTPS 302s them before
# the origin sees the request, so this host cannot serve the password login over
# plaintext regardless. It survives for the one path that bypasses the edge — a
# direct-to-origin :80 hit — where it 301s to https instead of dropping into the
# port-80 catch-all's 421 (both verified 2026-08-07). It cannot fight the edge,
# because anything arriving through the edge was already redirected there. Deleting
# it would not expose the login (the catch-all 421s), but it would turn a working
# upgrade into a confusing error for the direct path.
http://admin.mastermind-x.com {
	redir https://admin.mastermind-x.com{uri} permanent
}

admin.mastermind-x.com {
	encode zstd gzip

	# Reuse the generated research pages, but only behind the admin session. The
	# prefix is stripped before file lookup so every existing relative CSS, JS,
	# and data dependency continues to resolve without a second page build.
	handle_path /research-tools/* {
		forward_auth 127.0.0.1:8787 {
			uri /api/auth-check
			header_up X-Admin-Client-IP {remote_host}
		}
		root * /opt/macro/site.served
		header Cache-Control "private, no-store"
		file_server
	}

	handle {
		reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8787 {
			# {remote_host} is the verified TCP peer and header_up REPLACES any
			# caller-supplied X-Admin-Client-IP, so this value cannot be spoofed.
			# That half is deliberately UNCHANGED by the lockout fix below: it is what
			# stops a brute-forcer rotating buckets, and every identity decision in the
			# admin app is built on top of it.
			#
			# What does NOT hold is the claim this comment carried until 2026-08-07 —
			# that admin.* is "grey-cloud / direct-to-origin (no CDN), so the TCP peer
			# IS the real client". It is edge-proxied (see the block header), so for
			# every visitor arriving through the edge the peer is an EDGE IP: a probe
			# fired from a residential Mac landed here as 43.175.104.236.
			#
			# The live defect that followed — admin/auth.py keying its per-client login
			# lockout on this value, so ALL edge-borne traffic shared ONE bucket and any
			# anonymous client could lock the operator out of their own console — is
			# FIXED IN THE APP, not here. admin/edge_trust.py resolves the visitor behind
			# an ATTESTED edge peer and falls back to this peer otherwise, so nothing in
			# this block had to change.
			#
			# READ THAT MODULE BEFORE ADDING A header_up HERE. Probing THIS host
			# through the edge on 2026-08-07 (headers captured off the :8787 loopback hop)
			# found EO-Connecting-IP is the ONLY header the edge overwrites; EO-Client-IP,
			# True-Client-IP, X-Real-IP and CF-Connecting-IP all arrived carrying the
			# forged values a caller sent, and a clean edge request carries no
			# EO-Client-IP at all. Forwarding any of those as identity would reopen the
			# hole in the other direction — unlimited bucket rotation.
			#
			# The EO-Client-IP half of that is TRUE HERE AND FALSE ON www, where the
			# EdgeOne "Client IP Header" rule is active and the edge overwrites it (see
			# the global options block). Do not copy this paragraph to another vhost —
			# re-probe the zone you are actually editing.
			header_up X-Admin-Client-IP {remote_host}
		}
	}
	header {
		X-Robots-Tag "noindex, nofollow, noarchive"
		X-Frame-Options "DENY"
		X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"
		Referrer-Policy "no-referrer"
		Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000"
		-Server
	}
}

# ---- press properties (D14 W1.5) — mastermindx.ai + blog.mastermind-x.com ----
# COMMENTED OUT ON PURPOSE. This block is the SERVING half of the press cutover
# and it is inert until the day the properties go live.
#
# THE PAIRING (CI-enforced, tests/test_press_properties.py::the cutover sync
# guard): the commit that uncomments the block between the two marker lines
# below MUST be the same commit that sets `cutover: true` in config/press.yml,
# and vice versa. The test asserts BOTH directions, so forgetting either half
# turns the PR red rather than shipping a live vhost with no content routed to
# it (or a routed emit with nothing serving it).
#
# UNCOMMENT MECHANICALLY, do not hand-edit. The exact one-line sed lives in
# app/deploy/README.md § "Press properties (W1.5) — cutover runbook"; it is
# deliberately NOT quoted here, because a sed range written out in this comment
# is itself matched by that range and gets uncommented into a syntax error
# (found exactly that way, by validating the result). Every line between the
# markers starts with '#' at COLUMN 0 — the leading tabs live AFTER the '#' —
# so stripping one leading '#' from each interior line yields the intended
# config verbatim.
#
# DNS FIRST, THEN THIS. Order matters and it is not reversible in the other
# direction: point the A records at 146.190.142.17 (Spaceship DNS: mastermindx.ai
# @ and www; blog.mastermind-x.com), confirm with `dig`, THEN merge the cutover
# PR. Uncommenting before DNS resolves here is not dangerous — Caddy would simply
# retry ACME HTTP-01 until the records appear, and no visitor can reach a host
# that does not point at the box — but the pairing test forbids it anyway,
# because the config half without the DNS half is a claim nobody verified.
#
# REAL CERTIFICATES, NOT `tls internal`. These two are planned as direct A records
# to 146.190.142.17 with NO CDN in front, so a browser sees this box's certificate
# and it has to be a genuine Let's Encrypt one — ACME HTTP-01 on :80 works
# directly for them (ufw keeps 80/443 open to Anywhere). A `tls internal` here
# would serve a self-signed cert straight to a browser; the test asserts its
# absence. NB this paragraph used to cite them as "GREY-CLOUD / direct A records,
# exactly like admin.mastermind-x.com above" — admin is edge-proxied like every
# other host here (corrected 2026-08-07), so on cutover these two would be the
# FIRST direct hosts in the file, not a copy of an existing arrangement. Re-check
# that assumption at cutover rather than inheriting it.
#
# WHY THE http:// BLOCKS EXIST: the global `auto_https disable_redirects` (right
# for the CDN-fronted hosts, which must not fight the edge's own redirect) means
# Caddy adds NO automatic http->https upgrade. Without an explicit redirect these
# hosts would answer plain http from the port-80 catch-all as a 421. Caddy answers
# ACME HTTP-01 challenges BEFORE routing, so these redirects never interfere with
# issuance or renewal — the same ordering admin.* relies on, though for admin the
# challenge now arrives via the edge's HTTPS origin-pull rather than on :80.
#
# ONE CANONICAL HOST. The apex (mastermindx.ai) serves; www 301s to it. Serving
# BOTH names off one site block would put every page on two live URLs and rely
# entirely on rel=canonical to sort it out — a hint, not an instruction, and one
# that costs a crawl of every duplicate to even read. config/press.yml
# `base_url` is https://mastermindx.ai, so the redirect target is the same host
# the sitemap, the RSS feed and every JSON-LD `url` already name. www gets its
# own site block and therefore its own ACME certificate (a redirect still needs
# a valid cert to perform the redirect over https).
#
# HSTS STARTS SHORT ON PURPOSE: max-age=300, not a year. HSTS is the one header
# a browser will not let you take back — once a client has cached a long
# max-age it REFUSES plain http for that host for the full duration, so a
# certificate or DNS mistake on a brand-new domain is unreachable-by-design
# until the header expires. Five minutes is enough to be real and short enough
# to be survivable. Raise it to 31536000 after a clean week (runbook step 5).
#
# ROLLBACK: revert the cutover PR. That re-comments this block and puts
# `cutover: false` back in the same move, which is the point of pairing them.
# The DNS records can stay pointed here — with the block commented, those hosts
# fall through to the :80 catch-all / no TLS host and serve nothing.
#
# The served roots are published by app/deploy/update.sh, which rsyncs
# properties/news -> press_news.served and properties/research ->
# press_research.served with the same atomic-rename + --min-size=1 protections
# site.served uses (the 2026-07-03 white-page incident).
# PRESS-CUTOVER-BEGIN
#mastermindx.ai {
#	root * /opt/macro/press_news.served
#	encode zstd gzip
#	file_server {
#		index index.html
#	}
#	handle_errors {
#		rewrite * /404.html
#		file_server
#	}
#
#	@html path *.html /
#	header @html Cache-Control "public, max-age=300, must-revalidate"
#	@versioned {
#		path /assets/*
#		query v=*
#	}
#	header @versioned Cache-Control "public, max-age=31536000, immutable"
#	@feeds path /feed.xml /sitemap.xml /robots.txt
#	header @feeds Cache-Control "public, max-age=300, must-revalidate"
#
#	header {
#		# Ramp: raise to 31536000 once the property has served a clean week
#		# (runbook step). A long max-age on a new host is not revocable.
#		Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=300"
#		X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"
#		X-Frame-Options "DENY"
#		Referrer-Policy "strict-origin-when-cross-origin"
#		Content-Security-Policy "default-src 'self'; style-src 'self'; img-src 'self' data:; base-uri 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'; form-action 'self'"
#		-Server
#	}
#}
